<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  m365apps-remove-consumer.xml
  Removes an OEM-preinstalled CONSUMER Click-to-Run Office, as a separate pass
  before the enterprise install. Used by Install-M365Apps.ps1 only when
  -RemovePreinstalledConsumerOffice is set; the switch is off by default.

  Why this file exists
  ===================
  <RemoveMSI /> in m365apps-base.xml only removes Windows Installer versions of
  Office. Microsoft is explicit that it does not touch Click-to-Run:

    "RemoveMSI doesn't uninstall prior versions of Office, including Visio and
     Project, that use Click-to-Run as the installation technology. Uninstall
     those versions of Office through Control Panel or by running the Office
     Deployment Tool and using the Remove element in your configuration.xml."
    https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-365-apps/deploy/upgrade-from-msi-version

  So on hardware whose image ships a consumer Microsoft 365 SKU, that product
  remains registered next to O365ProPlusRetail after the enterprise install.
  Two consequences:

    1. ODT reconciles the existing install onto the configured channel and
       build rather than installing cleanly, since Click-to-Run supports one
       channel and version per device.
    2. The device carries two Office product registrations afterwards, which
       can confuse licensing and activation, and the default surgical
       uninstall (m365apps-remove.xml) leaves the consumer entry in place.

  Why it is a separate file rather than a <Remove> block in the base XML
  =====================================================================
  ODT documents <Remove> standalone and never alongside <Add>, so removal has
  to be its own setup.exe /configure pass. Install-M365Apps.ps1 runs this one
  first, then the base configuration.

  Scoping
  =======
  Deliberately NOT <Remove All="TRUE" />. This lists consumer SKUs explicitly
  so that a device which already has a legitimate enterprise Office, Visio or
  Project installed is left alone. Keep this list in step with
  $ConsumerProductIds in Install-M365Apps.ps1 - see docs/customization.md.

  Language elements are intentionally omitted: per Microsoft's ODT
  documentation, omitting the language attribute makes ODT discover and remove
  all installed languages for the product.
-->
<Configuration ID="m365apps-remove-consumer">

  <Remove>
    <Product ID="O365HomePremRetail" />
    <Product ID="O365SmallBusPremRetail" />
    <Product ID="O365BusinessRetail" />
    <Product ID="HomeBusinessRetail" />
    <Product ID="HomeStudentRetail" />
    <Product ID="PersonalRetail" />
  </Remove>

  <Property Name="FORCEAPPSHUTDOWN" Value="TRUE" />

  <Display Level="None" AcceptEULA="TRUE" />

</Configuration>
